{
  "children": [
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Efficient Monte Carlo sampling of Bayesian posteriors in high dimensions",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Title"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "A. Researcher¹, B. Supervisor¹, C. Collaborator²",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "¹ Department of Statistics, University of Somewhere; ² Institute of Applied Mathematics, ETH Zürich",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "Footnote"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Author"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Motivation",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Bayesian inference in high-dimensional parameter spaces is computationally challenging. Standard Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) methods suffer from slow mixing when the posterior has strong correlations or multiple modes. We propose a preconditioned Hamiltonian Monte Carlo (HMC) sampler that adapts its mass matrix online, achieving effective sample sizes 5–20× larger than the No-U-Turn Sampler (NUTS) for the same wall-clock time.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Method",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "We augment the target density ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\pi(\\theta)",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " with momentum variables ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "p \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, M)",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " and simulate the Hamiltonian",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "latex": "H(\\theta, p) = -\\log \\pi(\\theta) + \\tfrac{1}{2} p^\\top M^{-1} p.",
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "eq:hamiltonian",
      "type": "MathBlock"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The mass matrix ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "M",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " is updated every ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "K",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " leapfrog steps using a rank-one update from the sample covariance of the last ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "W",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " accepted states. The update converges to the posterior covariance and the sampler becomes asymptotically optimal in the Gaussian limit.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Key results",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "rows": [
        [
          "Problem (d)",
          "NUTS ESS/s",
          "Ours ESS/s",
          "Speedup"
        ],
        [
          "Logistic regression (50)",
          "420",
          "2 100",
          "5.0×"
        ],
        [
          "Gaussian process (200)",
          "38",
          "540",
          "14.2×"
        ],
        [
          "Hierarchical model (500)",
          "12",
          "240",
          "20.0×"
        ]
      ],
      "caption": "Effective sample size per second on three benchmarks.",
      "label": "tab:poster-results",
      "alignment": "lrrr",
      "style": "",
      "type": "Table"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The gains are most pronounced when the posterior has strong off-diagonal correlations, because the adapted mass matrix de-correlates the geometry for the leapfrog integrator.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Convergence guarantee",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Under mild regularity conditions on ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\pi",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": ", the adaptive chain satisfies a diminishing-adaptation criterion ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "roberts2007"
          ],
          "style": "citep",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": " and therefore preserves ergodicity. Specifically, if the update schedule satisfies",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "latex": "\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\|M_{k+1} - M_k\\|_F < \\infty,",
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "eq:diminishing",
      "type": "MathBlock"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "then the chain has the correct stationary distribution. Our rank-one schedule satisfies this by construction, since the covariance estimate converges almost surely.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Conclusion",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Online mass-matrix adaptation dramatically improves HMC efficiency in high dimensions.",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "The method is a drop-in replacement for NUTS in probabilistic programming frameworks.",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        },
